  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 101 AM CDT Thu Oct 15 2026\n\nAnother unseasonably hot day today with most locations remaining \nrain free due to ridging aloft. Although, a rogue shower or two \ncan't be ruled out.\n\nThe main weather event will be a strong cold front moving through S \nTX on Friday, finally bringing some relief from the heat. The cooler \nand drier conditions will be more noticeable Friday night. A \npleasant weekend is in store for S TX with highs in the 70s and lows \nin the 50s to low 60s. Temperatures begin to warm back up early next \nweek.\n\nTiming of the cold front is still somewhat uncertain. Models \nindicate a wind shift late Thursday night with the main blast \npushing through the area Friday morning. Friday's max temperature \nmay occur Friday morning. Most of S TX will have a 10-25% chance of \nshowers, but there is a higher chance up to 50% along the Rio \nGrande. The highest chance will be over the gulf waters where the \ndeepest moisture will reside. At this time, it looks like mainly \nshowers with a few thunderstorms possible.\n\nFriday is expected to be windy as high pressure builds across S TX \nbehind the cold front. A Wind Advisory may be needed, at least \ncloser to the coast. The strongest winds will be across the bays and \ncoastal waters with gale conditions expected Friday afternoon \nthrough early Saturday afternoon. Winds begin to relax Saturday \nafternoon and evening. \n\nAnother concern will be coastal flooding. The strong north to \nnortheast winds across the gulf waters in the wake of the cold front \nwill likely lead to minor coastal flooding. Seas are forecast to \nbuild to 8-13 feet, possibly up to 14 feet. Swell periods are \nforecast to increase to 8-9 seconds. The combination will be \nconducive for pushing water up to the dunes during times of high \ntide. A Coastal Flood Advisory will likely be needed by Friday night \nthrough Saturday, and maybe into Sunday. However, if the winds and \nseas are slightly higher than expected, a Coastal Flood Warning may \nbe warranted with water up to 3 feet above MSL during high tide. At \nthis time confidence is very low that we will see tides that high.",
